---

<DocsLayout
  title="AI Agents Plugin - fuku"
  description="fuku plugin for Claude Code and Codex: run, inspect, debug and stop local services from an agent."
  current={current}
  nav={nav}
  section="plugins"
  breadcrumb={[{ label: "Plugins", href: `${base}plugins/` }, { label: "AI agents", href: current }]}
>
  <h1>AI Agents Plugin</h1>
  <p class="lead">Let Claude Code or Codex run, inspect, debug and stop your local services with fuku.</p>

  <p>The plugin starts <code>fuku mcp</code>, an MCP server with six fuku tools. It also holds one Agent Skill named <code>fuku</code>. Both hosts load the same plugin.</p>
  <p>The server tells the agent how to run, inspect and stop services. The skill covers config edits: shared or local-only files, secrets, and applying a change. It tells the agent to use the tools and never run <code>fuku</code> or <code>curl</code> from the shell. It adds no slash command and no hook.</p>
  <p>It needs a fuku that has the <code>fuku mcp</code> command on <code>PATH</code>, and a project with a <code>fuku.yaml</code>.</p>

  <div class="section-eyebrow">Installation</div>
  <h2>Claude Code</h2>

  <p>In a Claude Code session, add the marketplace, then install the plugin:</p>

  <CodeTerminal code={`/plugin marketplace add tab/fuku
/plugin install fuku@fuku`} />

  <p><code>/plugin install</code> opens the plugin's details. Pick a scope there to install it.</p>
  <p>From your shell, the same steps are:</p>

  <CodeTerminal code={`claude plugin marketplace add tab/fuku
claude plugin install fuku@fuku`} />

  <p>A shell install loads in the next session, or after <code>/reload-plugins</code>.</p>

  <h2>Codex</h2>

  <p>From your shell, add the marketplace, then install the plugin:</p>

  <CodeTerminal code={`codex plugin marketplace add tab/fuku
codex plugin add fuku@fuku`} />

  <p>You can also install it from the plugin browser. Enter <code>/plugins</code> in the Codex CLI to open it.</p>
  <p>The plugin loads when you start a new chat or CLI session.</p>

  <div class="section-eyebrow">Upgrade</div>
  <h2>Claude Code</h2>

  <p>From your shell, refresh the marketplace, then update the plugin:</p>

  <CodeTerminal code={`claude plugin marketplace update fuku
claude plugin update fuku@fuku`} />

  <p>Start a new session after the upgrade.</p>

  <h2>Codex</h2>

  <p>From your shell, upgrade the marketplace, then add the plugin again:</p>

  <CodeTerminal code={`codex plugin marketplace upgrade fuku
codex plugin add fuku@fuku`} />

  <p>Start a new session after the upgrade.</p>

  <div class="section-eyebrow">How it works</div>
  <h2>The MCP server</h2>

  <p>The host starts <code>fuku mcp</code> over stdio when the agent session opens. It starts without a running fuku and without a valid <code>fuku.yaml</code>.</p>
  <p>The project is <code>CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR</code>, or the working directory. Every tool takes an optional <code>config</code> path, like <code>--config</code>.</p>
  <p>Tools take and return service names. The server loads the config fresh on a call that needs it, so a config edit during the session counts.</p>
  <p>Stdout carries only the protocol. Stderr carries a startup banner and one line per tool call: the tool, the outcome and the duration. The call line shows no arguments except a service name and action, and no results.</p>
  <p>The agent never sees the API token. The server sends it only to a loopback address of this project's fuku.</p>

  <h2>The tools</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>status</code></td><td>Reads the profile, the phase, the run mode and each service's state, PID, uptime and whether it watches files. Read-only</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>logs</code></td><td>Reads recent log lines, 100 by default and 1000 at most, and never follows. Name services to narrow it. Read-only</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>doctor</code></td><td>Runs the doctor report. It works while the agent fixes a broken <code>fuku.yaml</code>. Read-only</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>run</code></td><td>Starts a profile in the background, like <code>fuku run &lt;profile&gt; -d</code>, and returns its exit code and summary</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>stop</code></td><td>Stops the running profile, like <code>fuku stop</code>. It is marked destructive</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>service</code></td><td>Starts, stops or restarts one service and returns once it settles</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Attach before starting</h3>
  <p>Only one fuku runs per project. The developer may already run it.</p>
  <p>The agent calls <code>status</code> first. With no running fuku, <code>status</code> says so, and the agent may call <code>run</code>. With a running fuku, the agent attaches to it. A second <code>run</code> fails with the reason.</p>

  <h3>Start and stop</h3>
  <p><code>run</code> returns once every service runs, or once the start fails. After 50 seconds it answers <code>inProgress</code>, and the agent calls <code>status</code> to follow the start.</p>
  <p><code>stop</code> also stops leftover processes in the profile's service directories, including ones the agent did not start.</p>
  <p>The agent leaves fuku running when the task is done. It calls <code>stop</code> only when you ask for it, or to apply a config change to a detached fuku.</p>

  <h3>One-service actions</h3>
  <p><code>service</code> calls the <a href={`${base}docs/api/`}>REST API</a> with the body <code>{'{"wait": 45}'}</code>. It returns the state the service settled in: <code>running</code> with its PID, <code>stopped</code>, or <code>failed</code> with the last 20 log lines.</p>
  <p>When the wait ends first, it returns the current state with <code>inProgress: true</code>. The agent calls <code>status</code> to follow it.</p>
  <p>A service with <code>watching: true</code> restarts itself after a file change. After a code edit, the agent calls <code>status</code> until the watching service whose files it edited shows a new PID or <code>failed</code>, then tests it. It restarts only the services that do not watch.</p>
  <p>An unknown name is an error that lists the valid names.</p>
  <p>The API is off when <code>server.listen</code> is <code>""</code> or <code>none</code>, when <code>FUKU_API_DISABLED=1</code> is set, or when its port and the next 9 are all busy. Then <code>status</code> lists the services with state <code>unknown</code>, and <code>service</code> fails and says to use <code>stop</code> and <code>run</code>.</p>

  <h3>Config changes need a full restart</h3>
  <p>fuku reads its config once, when it starts. A <code>service</code> restart does not apply a config change.</p>
  <p><code>status</code> reports the <code>mode</code> of the running fuku: <code>tui</code>, <code>foreground</code> or <code>detached</code>. An older fuku reports <code>unknown</code>.</p>
  <p>After a change to <code>fuku.yaml</code> or the override, the agent calls <code>stop</code>, then <code>run</code>, only when the mode is <code>detached</code>. Otherwise fuku runs in your terminal: the agent does not stop it and tells you the change needs a restart.</p>
  <p>Shared changes go to <code>fuku.yaml</code>, local-only ones to <code>fuku.override.yaml</code>. The agent calls <code>doctor</code> after every edit.</p>
  <p>An experiment, a temporary config change to try something, goes only in <code>fuku.override.yaml</code>. The agent never creates a config file elsewhere. At the end it restores the override as it was, applies that like a config change and reports the keys it changed.</p>

  <h3>Stop rules</h3>
  <p>The agent stops or restarts fuku on your request, in every mode, or to apply a config change to a detached fuku. It does not stop the fuku that runs in your terminal to apply a config change; it tells you the change needs a restart.</p>
  <p>When fuku already serves the wanted profile and nothing needs a restart, the agent uses it as it is.</p>

  <h3>Missing tools</h3>
  <p>When the fuku tools are missing, the agent tells you to install fuku, put it on <code>PATH</code> and enable the plugin. It does not fall back to the shell.</p>

  <div class="section-eyebrow">Sandbox</div>
  <h2>Socket and network access</h2>

  <p>An agent sandbox may block the Unix socket and loopback HTTP that <code>fuku mcp</code> uses.</p>
  <p>A tool error about the socket or the API does not mean fuku is down. The agent asks you for local socket or network access, then retries.</p>
  <p>It does not start a second fuku and does not delete the socket because a call failed.</p>

